  <section id="decisions">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-header">
        <h2>Decisions Log</h2>
        <p>The calls, the reasoning, what happened next.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="decisions-list">

        <article class="decision">
          <div class="decision-meta">
            <span class="decision-date">May 2026</span>
            <span class="decision-outcome open">Open</span>
          </div>
          <h4>Build Boundless Horizons before raising.</h4>
          <p>The pattern of "raise on a deck, then build" is misaligned with how I actually move. Closing the loop the other way: ship a working v1 to a small group, see what compounds, then talk to capital with leverage instead of dependence. Cost: longer to scale. Upside: nothing to renegotiate later.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="decision">
          <div class="decision-meta">
            <span class="decision-date">Apr 2026</span>
            <span class="decision-outcome worked">Worked</span>
          </div>
          <h4>Renamed TourSync → TM Go.</h4>
          <p>TourSync had collision in search and was a mouthful in group SMS. TM Go is two syllables, queryable, and owns the .com tier. The migration cost was a weekend; the brand clarity has been worth it every week since.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="decision">
          <div class="decision-meta">
            <span class="decision-date">Mar 2026</span>
            <span class="decision-outcome open">Open</span>
          </div>
          <h4>Open-sourcing the LangGraph eval harness early.</h4>
          <p>Tempted to keep it private and let it be a moat. Decided the opposite: distribution beats moat when the tool itself is the demonstration of skill. Watching adoption to see if the bet is right.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="decision">
          <div class="decision-meta">
            <span class="decision-date">Feb 2026</span>
            <span class="decision-outcome worked">Worked</span>
          </div>
          <h4>Render over AWS for both startups.</h4>
          <p>AWS is the right answer at scale; it is the wrong answer at 0→1. Render lets me ship a stateful FastAPI service in an afternoon without writing IAM policy fan-fiction. We'll graduate when the cost of graduating is cheaper than the cost of not.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="decision">
          <div class="decision-meta">
            <span class="decision-date">Jan 2026</span>
            <span class="decision-outcome worked">Worked</span>
          </div>
          <h4>Killed TM Go's "events module" pre-launch.</h4>
          <p>Customers had asked. The feature would have shipped. But it dragged the core flow toward configurability and away from the SMS-first experience that was working. Cut it, refocused. Three customers later, no one missed it.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="decision">
          <div class="decision-meta">
            <span class="decision-date">Dec 2025</span>
            <span class="decision-outcome reversed">Reversed</span>
          </div>
          <h4>Tried to ship TM Go and SelfNVest in parallel sprints.</h4>
          <p>For three weeks I split focus 60/40, then 50/50, then 40/60. Both products got worse. Reversed in early Jan — TM Go takes priority, SelfNVest goes to maintenance mode. Lesson: parallel founders are rare for a reason.</p>
        </article>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

